TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R13","met":"partial","where":null},{"id":"R14","met":"no","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R15","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"A sun-synchronous orbit maintains approximately constant local solar time, not continuous sunlight. The claim that the satellite has a constant supply of sunlight and that the descending path is constantly sunlit is not established; eclipse periods depend on orbital geometry and local time of the node."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The 600 km altitude and 97.79° inclination are plausible for a sun-synchronous orbit, but no sensor, spectral bands, ground sample distance, signal-to-noise analysis, or water-depth capability is given to establish that fluid-lensing reef imagery is feasible."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report says a 250 km reef width motivates the altitude choice, but then proposes imaging sections without calculating swath, revisit, or time to cover the reef. The statement that low image resolution is desirable conflicts with the stated need for useful analysis."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"RAAN specifies the orientation of the orbital plane, not the launch facility's location. The initial RAAN and argument of latitude lack an epoch or specified local time of the ascending node, so the initial orbit geometry is not fully defined."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Mount Pleasant is identified as a radio observatory, but the report does not establish that it has spacecraft command and telemetry equipment, suitable frequencies, or a data link budget. No data volume or transfer-rate calculation supports the claim that contacts are sufficient."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The access table duplicates the same access as entries 14 and 15, so the reported access statistics may double-count a pass. Its multi-day output also does not establish a clear operational communication cadence."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"A drag sail is proposed without sizing, deployment reliability, or an estimated decay time from 600 km. The report therefore does not demonstrate that passive disposal meets an acceptable post-mission lifetime."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"The report selects a future Vega E rideshare concept rather than identifying a confirmed launch opportunity or showing that its deployment service can deliver this spacecraft into the specified orbit."},{"where":"Bibliography","issue":"Wikipedia is cited as a source despite the task explicitly rejecting it as a proper source. Several table citations numbered [50] and above do not correspond to entries in the bibliography, which contains only references [1]–[10]."}],"overall":"The report presents a coherent mission concept and covers most required design topics, with useful STK screenshots and access outputs. Its principal weaknesses are unverified reef-imaging feasibility, incomplete communications and disposal analyses, and presentation and referencing defects. The selected orbit is plausible, but several operational claims are unsupported or inaccurate.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The mission purpose, Australian conservation rationale, single-spacecraft choice, and likely users are explained. However, the operator and paying or operational customers are not clearly distinguished or assigned, and the mission rationale lacks supporting literature for some claims.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The spacecraft is intended to image and send data to ground stations for collation and analysis."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report addresses launch choice, orbit and elements, manoeuvres, ground station access, duration, and EOL, with STK outputs and screenshots. However, the mission design is weakly supported in key areas: payload and imaging feasibility, reef revisit, communications link, launch implementation, and EOL decay time are not quantified. Some orbit and sunlight claims are technically misleading.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"The orbit is circular and thus has no perigee."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Systems Tool Kit 11 (STK) was used to calculate access periods as seen below:"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"The most promising alternative is a ‘drag sail’ – a drag-enhancing deorbit device that deploys at the EOL."},{"where":"Image 3","quote":""}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion recaps the selected architecture and identifies a launch-vehicle contingency. It is relevant but mostly summarizes choices rather than drawing detailed conclusions from the evidence or acknowledging major uncertainties in imaging performance, coverage, and EOL.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"Thus, launch may need to be adjusted if the rocket is still not constructed by the proposed date."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a recognizable, logical structure and an executive summary, and the main text is generally understandable. Presentation is not professional: grammar and wording errors occur, figures and tables are not consistently numbered and titled, and the reference list is incomplete and inconsistent with some in-text citations; Wikipedia is used as a source.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Image 4","quote":""},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."}]}]}
